@page "/learn/virtualize-anchor-mode"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Lists that follow the newest item · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Lists that follow the newest item</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">New messages arrive at the bottom. Does your list show them, or leave them out of sight?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Interactive Server · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>Virtualize</code> has an <code>AnchorMode</code> parameter.
        <code>VirtualizeAnchorMode.End</code> follows new items at the end while you're at the bottom, and stops
        when you scroll up. .NET 10's <code>Virtualize</code> could show a long list, but never followed its end.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a support inbox. It loads 300 messages, #10900 to #11199, some of them long. From then on a new one
        arrives every 2.5 seconds, so by the time you look the count is already past 300. The list is a plain
        <code>Virtualize</code> in a 300px box, as .NET 10 had it.
    </p>
    <p>
        Under the list, a readout's first line says where you are. The second tallies each new message by where you
        were when it arrived: <b>At the bottom</b> (did it follow?) or <b>Scrolled up</b> (did it pull you down?).
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The inbox, with a plain Virtualize" Url="demo/anchor-mode/inbox/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: plain Virtualize" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            It opens at the top, so nothing has failed yet: the messages counted under <b>Scrolled up</b> arrived
            while you were up there. To see it fail, click in the list and press <b>End</b> (press it again if it stops
            short, or drag the list's scrollbar to the bottom) until the readout says <b>at the bottom</b>. Then wait
            for two or three messages. <b>At the bottom</b> says <b>0 followed</b>: the first new message lands below
            the fold, and <b>newer out of sight</b> climbs from there. The diagram below shows the same thing.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Virtualize keeps your scroll position</h2>
    <p>When a message is added, the list gets taller below you, and the scroll position stays where it was:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Adding a message to a list you've scrolled to the bottom</span>
        <pre class="snip">┌ the box, 300px ───────────┐
│ #11197                    │
│ #11198                    │
│ #11199                    │  <span class="ok">at the bottom</span>
└───────────────────────────┘
  #11200  <span class="dim">messages.Add(...)</span>        <span class="err">below the fold</span>
  #11201  <span class="dim">messages.Add(...)</span>        <span class="err">below the fold</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        That suits someone reading older messages, but not someone waiting for the next one.
        <code>Virtualize</code> can't tell them apart, so it never scrolls. In our .NET 10 repro, the gap under the
        view grew by one row with every message: 0, then 51, 102, 153px.
    </p>
    <p>
        Fig. 1 runs on .NET 11, and a <code>Virtualize</code> without <code>AnchorMode</code> still does this. Upgrading
        doesn't make an existing list follow its end; you opt in.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Anchor the list to its end</h2>
    <p>Add one parameter to the same <code>Virtualize</code>. You add items exactly as before.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;div class="feed" style="height: 300px; overflow-y: auto"&gt;
    &lt;Virtualize Items="messages" Context="m"
                <span class="hl">AnchorMode="VirtualizeAnchorMode.End"</span>&gt;
        ...
    &lt;/Virtualize&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        With <code>End</code>, the list follows new items only while you're at the bottom, within 2px. Scroll away
        and it stops, so it never pulls a reader back down. Blazor's own script does the scrolling: no JavaScript of
        yours, and no round trip per message. Here's the same inbox with it.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same inbox, anchored to the end" Url="demo/anchor-mode/inbox"
                Label=".NET 11: AnchorMode.End">
        <Caption>
            It opens on the newest message. Wait for two or three: <b>At the bottom</b> counts every one as
            <b>followed</b>. Now scroll up a little, even one wheel notch, and wait again: <b>Scrolled up</b> says
            <b>0 pulled you down</b>, and the message you're reading doesn't move. Scroll back to the very bottom, and
            it follows again.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>The two lists differ by one parameter. Here's the anchored one, then its page, then the .NET 10 one:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AnchorMode/MessageFeed.razor" Feature="virtualize-anchor-mode">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's list. Only the <code>AnchorMode</code> line is new. The other highlighted lines are marked
            <i>Not new</i> in their comments: they show what you keep as it was, the box with a height and the plain
            <code>Add</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AnchorMode/SupportInbox.razor" Feature="virtualize-anchor-mode">
        <Caption>
            The page is static. Only the list runs interactively, because following happens live. The highlighted
            render mode line isn't new either.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/AnchorMode/MessageFeedNet10.razor" Feature="virtualize-anchor-mode">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's list: the same messages and the same timer, without <code>AnchorMode</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Your own JavaScript, or no virtualizing</h2>
    <p>
        Apps tracked the bottom themselves: ask JavaScript whether the reader is at the bottom, add the message, then
        scroll down if they were.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways to follow the end in .NET 10, and what they cost</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">var atBottom = await JS.InvokeAsync&lt;bool&gt;(
    <span class="err">"chat.isAtBottom"</span>, scroller);
messages.Add(message);
StateHasChanged();
if (atBottom) await JS.InvokeVoidAsync(
    <span class="err">"chat.scrollToBottom"</span>, scroller);</pre>
                <span class="why">in our repro, it followed three messages; then a tall one landed 72px short, <code>isAtBottom</code> said no, and it stopped following for good</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a JS round trip per message, and it breaks on mixed heights</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@foreach (var message in messages)
{
    &lt;ChatMessage Message="message" /&gt;
}</pre>
                <span class="why">every message stays on the page, with the same JavaScript to scroll down</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a busy feed gets slower as the day goes on</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        <b>If your app has the first one, delete it when you upgrade.</b> In our test on .NET 11, the
        <code>isAtBottom</code>/<code>scrollToBottom</code> workaround stopped following at the first new message.
        Set <code>AnchorMode</code> instead.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>One parameter, and the list does the scrolling</h2>
    <p>
        Fig. 2 sets <code>End</code>, which follows a list that grows at the bottom. <code>VirtualizeAnchorMode</code>
        is a set of flags, so there are three other values. <code>Start</code>, the default, keeps a newest-first list
        at its top when items are inserted there; it doesn't follow appended items, which is why Fig. 1 doesn't
        follow. <code>Start | End</code> pins both, and <code>None</code> turns anchoring off.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>On .NET 10, <code>AnchorMode</code> builds, then the page returns a 500.</b> Razor passes the unknown attribute through with no warning, and at runtime <code>Virtualize</code> "does not have a property matching the name 'AnchorMode'".</li>
        <li><b>Write <code>VirtualizeAnchorMode.End</code>, not <code>End</code>.</b> A bare <code>AnchorMode="End"</code> doesn't compile.</li>
        <li><b>Loading rows with <code>ItemsProvider</code>? Set <code>ItemComparer</code> too.</b> Compare by id, or fresh objects from each query look like rows inserted above, and the list stops following. Analyzer BL0011 warns you.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
